  <p>
    Finasteride is the most effective medical treatment available for male pattern hair loss.
    It is also the treatment men are most anxious about, usually because the information online
    swings between clinics that do not mention side effects at all and forums where the harm
    sounds certain and permanent.
  </p>

  <p>
    Neither is accurate. Here is what is actually known, including the parts that are unresolved.
  </p>

  <h2>How these medicines work</h2>

  <p>
    Male pattern hair loss is driven by dihydrotestosterone, or DHT, acting on genetically
    sensitive follicles. Finasteride blocks one of the enzymes that converts testosterone to
    DHT, lowering DHT levels substantially. Dutasteride blocks both forms of that enzyme, so it
    is more potent and stays in the body considerably longer.
  </p>

  <p>
    In India, dutasteride is approved for prostate enlargement, and its use for hair loss is
    off-label. Off-label prescribing is legal and common, but you are entitled to know when it
    is happening.
  </p>

  <h2>Sexual side effects</h2>

  <p>
    This is the concern that brings most men to this page. Reduced libido, difficulty with
    erections, and reduced ejaculate volume are the reported effects.
  </p>

  <p>
    In the trials that led to approval, these were reported by roughly 2 to 4 percent of men
    taking finasteride, compared with about 2 percent taking a placebo. So the increase
    attributable to the medicine is small — but it is not zero, and being in the small
    percentage is not made better by the percentage being small.
  </p>

  <p>
    In most men who develop these symptoms, they resolve after stopping the medicine. Some
    resolve while still taking it.
  </p>

  <h2>Mood changes</h2>

  <p>
    Depression and mood changes have been reported often enough that regulators in several
    countries have added them to the product information. If you have a history of depression,
    say so before starting. If your mood changes after starting, treat it as a reason to stop
    and speak to your doctor, not something to push through.
  </p>

  <h2>Persistent symptoms after stopping</h2>

  <p>
    A number of men report sexual, cognitive or mood symptoms continuing after they stop taking
    finasteride. This is often called post-finasteride syndrome.
  </p>

  <p>
    An honest summary: the reports are real, in the sense that men are genuinely experiencing and
    describing these symptoms. Whether the medicine causes a distinct lasting syndrome is not
    settled. There is no agreed diagnostic definition, no established mechanism, and most of the
    evidence is from self-reported case series rather than controlled studies, which makes cause
    and effect very hard to separate. Regulators in some countries have added persistent sexual
    dysfunction to product labelling on the basis of these reports.
  </p>

  <p>
    What that means practically: this is not a reason to refuse treatment outright, and it is not
    something to dismiss. It is a reason to make the decision deliberately, to start at the lowest
    effective approach, and to stop early if something feels wrong rather than waiting.
  </p>

  <h2>Things people are not usually told</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>PSA testing.</strong> These medicines roughly halve PSA. Any doctor testing your
      prostate in future needs to know.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Pregnancy.</strong> Finasteride can harm a developing male fetus. Women who are or
      may become pregnant must not take it, and should not handle broken or crushed tablets.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Dutasteride stays much longer.</strong> Its long half-life means it clears from the
      body slowly, which matters if you need to stop.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Topical finasteride exists.</strong> Applied to the scalp, it produces far less
      systemic absorption. The evidence base is smaller than for tablets, but it is a reasonable
      option for men who want the benefit with lower systemic exposure.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How this is handled here</h2>

  <p>
    Before starting, you should expect a conversation about all of the above, not a prescription
    handed over at the end of a five-minute appointment. That conversation covers what the
    medicine can realistically achieve for your degree of hair loss, what the alternatives are
    including doing nothing, and what to do if something changes.
  </p>

  <p>
    If you develop symptoms, stop and get in touch. There are other routes to take, and no
    treatment for hair loss is important enough to continue through side effects you are unhappy
    with.
  </p>

  <p style="background:var(--mist);padding:1.1rem;border-radius:var(--radius)">
